Published: 2026-08-13T00:00:00.000Z

The c.text() method has a fast path that's worth studying:
src/context.ts#L695-L707
text :  TextRespond  =  (   text :  string ,   arg ?:  ContentfulStatusCode  |  ResponseOrInit ,   headers ?:  HeaderRecord ) :  ReturnType < TextRespond >  =>  {   return  ! this .#preparedHeaders  &&  ! this .#status  &&  ! arg  &&  ! headers  &&  ! this .finalized     ?  ( new  Response (text)  as  ReturnType < TextRespond >)     :  ( this . #newResponse (         text,         arg,         setDefaultContentType ( TEXT_PLAIN , headers)       )  as  ReturnType < TextRespond >) }
When no headers have been set via c.header() , no status was customized, no extra arguments were passed, and the response hasn't been finalized — the most common case for simple handlers — Hono returns new Response(text) directly. No header merging, no status code lookup, no #newResponse machinery. Just a bare Web Standards Response.
flowchart TD
    A["c.text('Hello')"] --> B{Any prepared headers?}
    B -->|No| C{Custom status?}
    C -->|No| D{Extra args?}
    D -->|No| E{Already finalized?}
    E -->|No| F["new Response(text) — FAST PATH"]
    B -->|Yes| G["#newResponse(text, ...) — FULL PATH"]
    C -->|Yes| G
    D -->|Yes| G
    E -->|Yes| G
The res setter is where header merging gets sophisticated. When middleware sets headers before the handler creates the response, those headers need to be merged. But Set-Cookie headers require special treatment — they can't be merged with headers.set() because that overwrites previous cookies:
src/context.ts#L414-L434
set  res (_res: Response  |  undefined ) {   if  ( this .#res  &&  _res) {     _res  =  createResponseInstance (_res.body, _res)     for  ( const  [ k ,  v ]  of  this .#res.headers. entries ()) {       if  (k  ===  'content-type' ) {         continue       }       if  (k  ===  'set-cookie' ) {         const  cookies  =  this .#res.headers. getSetCookie ()         _res.headers. delete ( 'set-cookie' )         for  ( const  cookie  of  cookies) {           _res.headers. append ( 'set-cookie' , cookie)         }       }  else  {         _res.headers. set (k, v)       }     }   }   this .#res  =  _res   this .finalized  =  true }
The getSetCookie() API returns an array of individual cookie strings, avoiding the concatenation issue that plagued the old getAll('set-cookie') approach. Hono deletes the existing set-cookie header, then appends each cookie individually.
Tip: If you're setting cookies in middleware and in a handler, both will be preserved. But watch out for setting Content-Type in middleware — it gets skipped during merge (line 419) because the handler's content type takes priority.

## [Context and HonoRequest · Hono](https://hono.wikipilot.dev/how-it-works/context)

Hono v4.13.1
⌘K
Every handler receives a Context ( c ), built once per request in #dispatch . Context ( src/context.ts ) owns the response side and the per-request variable map; HonoRequest ( src/request.ts ) wraps the raw Request with parameter and body helpers. Both are engineered around one idea: do nothing until a handler actually asks.

Laziness is the design

c.req does not exist until the first access -- the getter constructs it on demand from the raw request and the router's match result:
src/context.ts
get req(): HonoRequest<P, I['out']> {
  this.#req ??= new HonoRequest(this.#rawRequest, this.#path, this.#matchResult)
