input.prompt:
You are implementing a focused bug fix in the Hono repo at:
<workspace>

SCOPE — you may edit ONLY these two files:
- src/context.ts
- src/context.test.ts

Do not touch any other files. Do NOT run git add/stage/commit. Leave changes unstaged.

BUG
In `Context` (src/context.ts), the `set res(_res: Response | undefined)` setter currently, when context headers already exist, deletes all `set-cookie` headers from the newly assigned `Response` and re-appends only the context's cookies. So if middleware already set a cookie on `c.res` (or via `c.header('Set-Cookie', ...)` into `#preparedHeaders`) and a handler returns a new `Response` with its own `Set-Cookie`, the handler's cookie is lost. Both must survive.

Currently (lines ~414-434):
```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type') {
          continue
        }
        if (k === 'set-cookie') {
          const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
          _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
          for (const cookie of cookies) {
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        } else {
          _res.headers.set(k, v)
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

REQUIRED BEHAVIOR
1. If either `this.#res` or `this.#preparedHeaders` has headers, and `_res` is provided, merge them into the provided Response (create a fresh mutable Response via `createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)` first).
2. `set-cookie` headers must be preserved from BOTH sides. Expected ordering: context cookies first, then the new Response's cookies (this matches upstream's intended semantics).
3. Avoid duplicate identical cookie strings: when the context response was built by Hono itself (e.g. `c.header('Set-Cookie','a=1'); c.res = c.text('Hi')`), the new Response already contains the context cookie, so blindly appending would duplicate it. Exact-string duplicate suppression is required; don't lose distinct cookies.
4. Keep the existing semantics for normal headers: skip context `content-type` (the new Response's content-type wins); apply other context headers with `.set(k, v)` so context headers retain their current precedence.
5. If `_res` is undefined, keep current behavior: assign `this.#res = _res` and set `this.finalized = true`.

SUGGESTED IMPLEMENTATION (adjust as needed, keep style/prettier of the repo — no semicolons):
```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    const contextHeaders = this.#res?.headers ?? this.#preparedHeaders
    if (contextHeaders && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)

      // Both the context and the new Response may have Set-Cookie headers. Preserve both.
      const contextCookies = contextHeaders.getSetCookie()
      const responseCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
      if (contextCookies.length || responseCookies.length) {
        _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
        const appended = new Set<string>()
        for (const cookie of [...contextCookies, ...responseCookies]) {
          if (!appended.has(cookie)) {
            appended.add(cookie)
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        }
      }

      for (const [k, v] of contextHeaders.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
          continue
        }
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```
Do not introduce new dependencies or abstract helpers; keep the change local to the setter.

TESTS (src/context.test.ts)
Read the existing `describe('Context header', ...)` block around lines 400-540 first. Then:
A. Update the existing test `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to c.res'` (~line 430). Its old expectations encode the buggy behavior (the new response's cookie is dropped). Change the final two assertions to expect all three cookies in order: context cookies first, then the new response's cookie:
```ts
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie().length).toBe(3)
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual([...cookies, ...newCookies])
```
B. Add a regression test in the same describe block that models middleware having already set a cookie on `c.res`, then a new Response with its own Set-Cookie being assigned:
```ts
  it('Should keep cookies set on the context and a newly assigned Response', () => {
    c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', 'middleware=1; Path=/')
    const res = new Response('ok', {
      headers: {
        'set-cookie': 'handler=1; Path=/',
      },
    })
    c.res = res
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual([
      'middleware=1; Path=/',
      'handler=1; Path=/',
    ])
  })
```
C. Add a regression test for the prepared-headers path (middleware used setCookie before #res existed):
```ts
  it('Should keep prepared cookies when assigning a raw Response', () => {
    setCookie(c, 'prepared', '1', { path: '/' })
    c.res = new Response(null, {
      headers: {
        'set-cookie': 'handler=1; Path=/',
      },
    })
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual(['prepared=1; Path=/', 'handler=1; Path=/'])
  })
```
D. Add a no-duplication test:
```ts
  it('Should not duplicate set-cookie when assigning a context-built Response', () => {
    c.header('Set-Cookie', 'a=1')
    c.res = c.text('Hi')
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual(['a=1'])
  })
```

VERIFICATION
Run exactly:
`pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts`
It must pass with no failures. If a new test reveals an issue, fix the setter (not the test expectations) unless the expectation itself is wrong. Also run `pnpm exec vp test --run src/hono.test.ts` to check for regressions and report the result.

FINAL RESPONSE must include:
- `file:line` references for the changed setter and each test.
- The exact test commands run and their pass/fail counts.
- Any behavior nuance you noticed (e.g., duplicate suppression, prepared-header merge).

